// A Linux client's SYN (mss 1460, sackOK, nop, wscale 7, without timestamps)
// gets Zhida's SYN-ACK; the client's bare ACK completes the handshake; the client's RST
// closes the connection, which a SYN-ACK to a new SYN on the same 4-tuple proves.
// The bare ACK is observable: sending the SYN-ACK (one sequence number) starts the
// retransmit timer at the RTO (2838-2843), initially 1000 ms (RTTE_INITIAL_RTO, 144; 184).
// The client then waits 1.5 s before sending data. Had the ACK been ignored, the SYN-ACK
// would still be unacknowledged and Zhida would retransmit it at about 1 s, an
// unexpected packet that fails the script. The echo of 10 bytes alone would not prove it:
// the data segment carries the same ACK.
// Zhida's SYN-ACK, from smoltcp 0.14.0 src/socket/tcp.rs:
// - window 65535: SYNs carry the unscaled rx window capped at u16::MAX (2603-2604); the
//   EchoServer rx buffer is 128 KiB.
// - mss 1460: ip_mtu 1500 - 20 - 20 (2779-2780); the option is not reduced for timestamps.
// - wscale 2: echoed because the SYN has wscale (1923, 2611); shift is
//   bit_length(131072) - 16, i.e. 2 (587, 608).
// - sackOK: echoed because the SYN has sackOK (1922, 2610).
// - The peer's SYN omits timestamps, so Socket::process disables the connection's
//   timestamp generator. Zhida sends no timestamp option on any segment.
// - Option order is mss, wscale, sackOK, then EOL padding to a 4-byte multiple:
//   4 + 3 + 2 = 9 option bytes, padded to 12 with three EOL bytes. Later segments
//   without SACK blocks have no options (src/wire/tcp.rs header_len and emit).
// - ISN: any; patched packetdrill maps each handshake's SYN-ACK sequence number to 0.
// The echo: see echo-and-close.pkt (delayed ACK piggybacked on PSH data, window 32768).
// The RST moves the socket to Closed (1895-1900); EchoServer::step listens again
// (crates/tap-echo/src/lib.rs 96-102). Had the RST been dropped, the SYN would meet an
// established connection, which drops a segment without ACK (1660-1663, the known bug in
// known-bugs/syn-on-established.pkt), and packetdrill would time out waiting for the
// SYN-ACK. The 100 ms gap keeps the RST and the SYN out of one turn's ingress (see
// echo-and-close.pkt: smoltcp 0.14 has no accept backlog, so with one listener a SYN in
// the RST's turn gets an RST; temporary, until Zhida's smoltcp fork). The second
// SYN-ACK's fresh ISN is mapped to 0 for this handshake (see echo-and-close.pkt).
// Window-update ACK after the handshake: the SYN-ACK window is unscaled, capped 65535;
// the scaled allowance, 90 frames x 1460 B capped by the 128 KiB buffer = 131072
// (32768 << 2), is >= 2 x 65535, so Zhida sends one pure window-update ACK.

0     < S 0:0(0) win 64240 <mss 1460,nop,nop,sackOK,nop,wscale 7>
+0    > S. 0:0(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460,wscale 2,sackOK,eol,eol,eol>
+0    < . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 502
+0    > . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 32768
+1.5  < P. 1:11(10) ack 1 win 502
+0    > P. 1:11(10) ack 11 win 32768
+0    < R. 11:11(0) ack 11 win 0

+0.1  < S 5000:5000(0) win 64240 <mss 1460,nop,nop,sackOK,nop,wscale 7>
+0    > S. 0:0(0) ack 5001 win 65535 <mss 1460,wscale 2,sackOK,eol,eol,eol>
